  date         = {2022-12-14},
  abstract     = {Deep brain stimulation (DBS) to the fornix is an
                  investigational treatment for patients with mild Alzheimer's
                  Disease. Outcomes from randomized clinical trials have shown
                  that cognitive function improved in some patients but
                  deteriorated in others. This could be explained by variance in
                  electrode placement leading to differential engagement of
                  neural circuits. To investigate this, we performed a post-hoc
                  analysis on a multi-center cohort of 46 patients with DBS to
                  the fornix (NCT00658125, NCT01608061). Using normative
                  structural and functional connectivity data, we found that
                  stimulation of the circuit of Papez and stria terminalis
                  robustly associated with cognitive improvement (R = 0.53, p <
                  0.001). On a local level, the optimal stimulation site resided
                  at the direct interface between these structures (R = 0.48, p
                  < 0.001). Finally, modulating specific distributed brain
