  date         = {2019},
  abstract     = {The striatum is a critical component of the brain that
                  controls motor, reward, and executive function. This ancient
                  and phylogenetically conserved structure forms a central hub
                  where rapid instinctive, reflexive movements and behaviors in
                  response to sensory stimulation or the retrieval of emotional
                  memory intersect with slower planned motor movements and
                  rational behaviors. This review emphasizes two distinct
                  pathways that begin in the thalamus and converge in the
                  striatum to differentially affect movements, behaviors, and
                  decision making. The convergence of excitatory glutamatergic
