  date         = {2015},
  abstract     = {Temporally coordinated signals at gamma frequencies and higher
                  are often used to study inter-regional communication in brain
                  networks, but interpreting mechanisms from population measures
                  can be troublesome. The authors discuss the physiological
                  origins of gamma coherence and suggest ways to decipher its
                  roles in neural function. Neural oscillations have been
                  measured and interpreted in multitudinous ways, with a variety
                  of hypothesized functions in physiology, information
                  processing and cognition. Much attention has been paid in
                  recent years to gamma-band (30–100 Hz) oscillations and
                  synchrony, with an increasing interest in 'high gamma' (>100
                  Hz) signals as mesoscopic measures of inter-regional
