  pages        = {223--241},
  date         = {2013},
  abstract     = {Dual-process and dual-system theories in both cognitive and
                  social psychology have been subjected to a number of recently
                  published criticisms. However, they have been attacked as a
                  category, incorrectly assuming there is a generic version that
                  applies to all. We identify and respond to 5 main lines of
                  argument made by such critics. We agree that some of these
                  arguments have force against some of the theories in the
                  literature but believe them to be overstated. We argue that
                  the dual-processing distinction is supported by much recent
                  evidence in cognitive science. Our preferred theoretical
                  approach is one in which rapid autonomous processes (Type 1)
