  journaltitle = {Front. Comput. Neurosci.},
  volume       = {14},
  pages        = {554097},
  date         = {2020},
  abstract     = {Despite the recent progress in AI powered by deep learning in
                  solving narrow tasks, we are not close to human intelligence
                  in its flexibility, versatility, and efficiency. Efficient
                  learning and effective generalization come from inductive
                  biases, and building Artificial General Intelligence (AGI) is
                  an exercise in finding the right set of inductive biases that
                  make fast learning possible while being general enough to be
                  widely applicable in tasks that humans excel at. To make
                  progress in AGI, we argue that we can look at the human brain
                  for such inductive biases and principles of generalization. To
                  that effect, we propose a strategy to gain insights from the
