                  for its absence. This might seem strange, for an institution
                  whose public health role has been forged through crisis—from
                  the thalidomide tragedy and the scandal of HIV-infected blood
                  supplies, to “mad cow disease” and the underwhelming H1N1
                  influenza pandemic. A closer review of the EU’s health
                  governance, however, reveals it to have performed exactly as
