                  research agenda from the margins}},
  author       = {Hanaček, Ksenija and Roy, Brototi and Avila, Sofia and Kallis,
                  Giorgos},
  journaltitle = {Ecol. Econ.},
  volume       = {169},
  pages        = {106495},
  date         = {2020},
  abstract     = {Research by ecological economists on degrowth is a flourishing
                  field. Existing research has focused on limits to (green)
                  growth and on economic alternatives for prospering without
                  growth. Future research, we argue here, should pay more
                  attention to, and be written, from the “margins” – that is
                  from the point of view of those marginalized in the growth
                  economy. We conduct a comprehensive systematic review of the
                  prevalent themes in the existing literature on the ecological
                  economics of degrowth, and its engagements with North-South
                  relations and gender issues. The analysis identifies seven
                  research areas where ecological economics can better integrate
                  these matters, namely: the study of post-growth policies for
                  the Global South; the unequal exchanges that sustain an
                  imperial mode of living; the deconstruction of ecological
                  economic concepts that reproduce problematic Western or
                  gendered assumptions; the study of the clash of metabolisms in
                  peripheries of the Global South; the metabolism of care-work
                  in growth economies; the leading role of women in ecological
                  distribution conflicts, and the reproduction of gender
                  inequalities in alternative post-growth spaces. We propose
