                  emotional events that comprise its category (e.g. “fear” is
                  distinct from instances of fear). Over the past several years,
                  research has dissolved these artificial boundaries, suggesting
                  instead that conceptual construction is a domain-general
                  process—a process by which the brain makes meaning of the
                  world. The brain constructs emotion concepts, but also
                  cognitions and perceptions, all in the service of guiding
                  action. In this view, concepts are multimodal constructions,
                  dynamically prepared from a set of highly variable instances.
                  This approach obviates old questions (e.g. how does cognition
                  regulate emotion?) but generates new ones (e.g. how does a
                  brain learn emotion concepts?). In this paper, we review this
                  constructionist, predictive coding account of emotion,
                  considering its implications for health and well-being,
                  culture and development.}
}

@ARTICLE{Hohwy2012-zh,
