  pages        = {275--280},
  date         = {2019},
  abstract     = {MR-guided focused ultrasound (MRgFUS) can temporarily
                  permeabilize the blood-brain barrier (BBB), noninvasively, to
                  allow therapeutics access to the central nervous system.
                  However, its secondary and potential neuromodulation effects
                  are not well understood. We aimed to characterize the
                  functional impact of MRgFUS BBB opening in human subjects,
                  based the phase I trial in patients with Alzheimer's disease.
                  We analyzed for changes in bilateral frontoparietal networks
                  in resting state functional MRI from five subjects after BBB
                  opening in the right prefrontal lobe. We found a transient
                  functional connectivity decrease within only the ipsilateral
                  frontoparietal network that was recovered by the next day.
                  Additionally, baseline to month three comparisons did not
                  reveal any significant differences from matched-controls from
