  journaltitle = {Mov. Disord.},
  date         = {2020},
  abstract     = {Beta‐frequency oscillations (13–30 Hz) are a subthalamic
                  hallmark in patients with Parkinson's disease, and there is
                  increased interest in their utility as an intraoperative
                  marker. The objectives of this study were to assess whether
                  beta activity measured directly from macrocontacts of deep
                  brain stimulation leads could be used (a) as an intraoperative
                  electrophysiological approach for guiding lead placements and
                  (b) for physiologically informed stimulation delivery. Every
                  millimeter along the surgical trajectory, local
                  field‐potential data were collected from each macrocontact,
                  and power spectral densities were calculated and visualized (n
                  = 39 patients). This was done for online intraoperative
                  functional mapping and post hoc statistical analyses using 2
                  methods: generating distributions of spectral activity along
                  surgical trajectories and direct delineation (presence versus
