                  neuroimaging) and uses a common metaphor to describe
                  decision-making across multiple levels of explanation. As
                  such, ecologically valid behavioral paradigms closely mirror
                  the physical mechanisms of reward processing. Third,
                  neuroeconomics provides a platform for investigators from
                  neuroscience, economics, psychiatry, and social and clinical
                  psychology to develop a common language for studying
                  reward-related decision making in psychiatric disorders.
                  Therefore, neuroeconomics can provide promising candidate
