  pages        = {1749--1775},
  date         = {2020},
  abstract     = {Predictive processing has recently been advanced as a global
                  cognitive architecture for the brain. I argue that its
                  commitments concerning the nature and format of cognitive
                  representation are inadequate to account for two basic
                  characteristics of conceptual thought: first, its
                  generality—the fact that we can think and flexibly reason
                  about phenomena at any level of spatial and temporal scale and
