  issue        = {9},
  pages        = {2306--2311},
  date         = {2013-06},
  abstract     = {Axial symptoms such as freezing of gait and falls are common
                  manifestations of advanced Parkinson's disease (PD) and are
                  partially responsive to medical treatment. High-frequency
                  (≥130 Hz) deep brain stimulation (DBS) of the subthalamic
                  nucleus (STN) is highly efficacious in ameliorating
                  appendicular symptoms in PD. However, it is typically less
                  effective in improving axial symptomatology, especially in the
                  long term. We have studied the effects of low-frequency
                  stimulation (LFS) (≤80 Hz) for improving speech, gait and
                  balance dysfunction in the largest patient population to date.
                  PD patients with bilateral STN-DBS and resistant axial
                  symptoms were switched from chronic 130 Hz stimulation to LFS
                  and followed up to 4 years. Primary outcome measures were
                  total motor UPDRS scores, and axial and gait subscores before
                  and after LFS. Bivariate analyses and correlation coefficients
                  were calculated for the different conditions. Potential
                  predictors of therapeutic response were also investigated.
                  Forty-five advanced PD patients who had high frequency
                  stimulation (HFS) for 39.5 ± 27.8 consecutive months were
                  switched to LFS. LFS was kept on for a median period of 111.5
                  days before the assessment. There was no significant
                  improvement in any of the primary outcomes between HFS and
