  date         = {2008},
  abstract     = {Motor imagery is a widely used paradigm for the study of
                  cognitive aspects of action control, both in the healthy and
                  the pathological brain. In this paper we review how motor
                  imagery research has advanced our knowledge of behavioral and
                  neural aspects of action control, both in healthy subjects and
