  pages        = {209},
  date         = {2018},
  abstract     = {Awe differs from common positive emotions, triggered by vast
                  stimuli, and characterized by a need for accommodation (NFA).
                  Although studies have revealed the downstream effects of awe
                  experience, little is known about the neural basis of
                  dispositional awe. In the current study, we determined the
                  neural correlation of dispositional awe by using voxel-based
                  morphometry (VBM) in 42 young healthy adults, as measured by
                  the Dispositional Positive Emotion Scale (DPES). Results
                  revealed that the dispositional awe score was negatively
                  associated with the regional gray matter volume (rGMV) in the
                  anterior cingulate cortex (ACC), middle/posterior cingulate
                  cortex (MCC/PCC) and middle temporal gyrus (MTG). These
                  results suggest that individual differences in dispositional
                  awe involve multiple brain regions related to attention,
