  volume       = {43},
  issue        = {45},
  pages        = {7565--7574},
  date         = {2023-11-08},
  abstract     = {The ability to store information about the past to dynamically
                  predict and prepare for the future is among the most
                  fundamental tasks the brain performs. To date, the problems of
                  understanding how the brain stores and organizes information
                  about the past (memory) and how the brain represents and
                  processes temporal information for adaptive behavior have
                  generally been studied as distinct cognitive functions. This
                  Symposium explores the inherent link between memory and
                  temporal cognition, as well as the potential shared neural
                  mechanisms between them. We suggest that working memory and
                  implicit timing are interconnected and may share overlapping
                  neural mechanisms. Additionally, we explore how temporal
