  pages        = {026113},
  date         = {2009-06},
  abstract     = {Parkinson's disease (PD) is a common, debilitating
                  neurodegenerative disease. Gait disturbances are a frequent
                  cause of disability and impairment for patients with PD. This
                  article provides a brief introduction to PD and describes the
                  gait changes typically seen in patients with this disease. A
                  major focus of this report is an update on the study of the
                  fractal properties of gait in PD, the relationship between
                  this feature of gait and stride length and gait variability,
                  and the effects of different experimental conditions on these
                  three gait properties. Implications of these findings are also
                  briefly described. This update highlights the idea that while
                  stride length, gait variability, and fractal scaling of gait
