  journaltitle = {Immunity},
  publisher    = {Elsevier},
  volume       = {0},
  issue        = {0},
  date         = {2021-11-22},
  abstract     = {SummaryPeople living with HIV (PLWH) are at increased risk for
                  developing skin and mucosal malignancies despite systemic
                  reconstitution of CD4+ T cells upon antiretroviral therapy
                  (ART). The underlying mechanism of chronic tissue-related
                  immunodeficiency in HIV is unclear. We found that skin CD4+
                  tissue-resident memory T (Trm) cells were depleted after HIV
                  infection and replenished only upon early ART initiation. TCR
                  clonal analysis following early ART suggested a systemic
                  origin for reconstituting CD4+ Trm cells. Single-cell RNA
                  sequencing in PLWH that received late ART treatment revealed a
                  loss of CXCR3+ Trm cells and a tolerogenic skin immune
                  environment. Human papilloma virus-induced precancerous lesion
                  biopsies showed reduced CXCR3+ Trm cell frequencies in the
                  mucosa in PLWH versus HIV− individuals. These results reveal
                  an irreversible loss of CXCR3+ Trm cells confined to skin and
                  mucosa in PLWH who received late ART treatment, which may be a
                  precipitating factor in the development of HPV-related cancer.},
