  issue        = {7},
  pages        = {783--788},
  date         = {2015-07},
  abstract     = {BACKGROUND: To deal with processing-time in the nervous
                  system, visuomotor control requires anticipation. An index for
                  such anticipation is provided by the 'flash-lag illusion' in
                  which moving objects are perceived ahead of static objects
                  while actually being in the same place. We investigated the
                  neurophysiological relation between visuomotor anticipation
                  and motor velocity in Parkinson's disease (PD) and controls.
                  METHODS: Motor velocity was assessed by the number of
                  keystrokes in 30s ('kinesia score') and visuomotor
                  anticipation in a behavioural flash-lag paradigm while
                  electroencephalography data was obtained. PD patients (n = 24)
                  were divided in a 'PDslow' and a 'PDfast' group based on
                  kinesia score. RESULTS: The PDslow group had a lower kinesia
                  score than controls (resp. 40.3 ± 1.7 and 64.9 ± 4.6, p <
