                  despite advances in cognitive neuroscience research and a
                  large public and media interest, there has been no unifying
                  computational and anatomical account of the cause of alien
                  limb movements. Here, we extend Bayesian brain principles to
                  propose that alien limb is a disorder of 'predictive
                  processing' in hierarchical sensorimotor brain networks.
                  Specifically, we suggest that alien limb results from
                  predictions about action outcomes that are afforded unduly
                  high precision. The principal mechanism for this abnormally
                  high precision is an impairment in the relay of input from
                  medial regions, predominantly the supplementary motor area
                  (SMA), which modulate the precision of lateral brain regions
                  encoding the predicted action outcomes. We discuss potential
