  pages     = {223--239},
  date      = {2019},
  abstract  = {Although "resilience" has become part of the popular and clinical
               lexicon, the concept is not easily defined. What degree of risk
               must families overcome to demonstrate resilience? Is resilience
               different from successful coping? What does resilience look like
               at the individual, family, and other ecosystem levels? Conceptual
               work in recent years has aimed to bring clarity to such issues,
               particularly in the field of family resilience. This chapter
               continues to refine the family resilience model as an overarching
               conceptual guide to studying family resilience. It builds on
               earlier conceptualizations of protection, vulnerability,
               adaptation, and family adaptive systems (FAS) that represent key
               areas of family functioning. The chapter argues that part of
               family adaptive systems' protective function can come through
               routines and rituals. It provides examples of how that might
               work, including examples that hold potential to be targeted in
