                  shape but not on smoking during follow-up and family history
                  of intracranial aneurysms in 1,507 patients with 1,909
                  unruptured intracranial aneurysms and used aneurysm growth as
                  outcome. With aneurysm-based multivariable Cox regression
                  analysis, we determined predictors for aneurysm growth, which
                  were presented as a risk score to calculate 3-year and 5-year
                  risks for aneurysm growth by risk factor status. RESULTS:
                  Aneurysm growth occurred in 257 patients (17\%) and 267
                  aneurysms (14\%) during 5,782 patient-years of follow-up.
                  Predictors for aneurysm growth were earlier subarachnoid
                  hemorrhage, location of the aneurysm, age >60 years,
                  population, size of the aneurysm, and shape of the aneurysm
                  (ELAPSS). The 3-year growth risk ranged from 42\% and the
