  pages        = {e2200931119},
  date         = {2022-05-17},
  abstract     = {SignificanceBidirectional communication between the
                  hippocampus and other brain areas via sharp wave ripples
                  (SPW-Rs) has been hypothesized to play an important role in
                  cognitive functions. However, brain-wide coupling to SPW-Rs
                  has been difficult to study at high temporal and spatial
                  resolution. Here, we show that SPW-Rs coincide with transient
                  brain-wide increase of functional connectivity. The diversity
                  in SPW-R features was related to slower excitability
                  fluctuations in cortical and subcortical areas. In return,
                  SPW-Rs correlated with the timing, sign, and magnitude of
                  partner structure responses. Our findings provide the most
