               What Do We Not Know?},
  author    = {Summerfield, C and Blangero, A},
  editor    = {Dreher, Jean-Claude and Tremblay, Léon},
  booktitle = {Decision Neuroscience},
  publisher = {Academic Press},
  location  = {San Diego},
  pages     = {149--162},
  date      = {2017-01-01},
  abstract  = {Perceptual decisions occur when sensory inputs are converted to
               discrete categorical variables. The neural and computational
               mechanisms by which rodents, monkeys, and humans make perceptual
               decisions have been intensively studied since 1989. Here, we
               address five questions that continue to raise controversy,
               summarizing research that has asked (1) how information is
               integrated to form a categorical choice, (2) what computations
               are carried out in cortical neurons during perceptual decisions,
               (3) how perceptual decision-making can be studied using cognitive
               neuroimaging techniques, (4) how we decide when to draw our
               decisions to a conclusion, and (5) how perceptual decisions are
