  date         = {2015-10-01},
  abstract     = {Deep brain stimulation (DBS) is a neurosurgical intervention
                  popularised in movement disorders such as Parkinson’s disease,
                  and also reported to improve symptoms of epilepsy, Tourette’s
                  syndrome, obsessive compulsive disorders and cluster headache.
                  Since the 1950s, DBS has been used as a treatment to relieve
                  intractable pain of several aetiologies including post stroke
                  pain, phantom limb pain, facial pain and brachial plexus
                  avulsion. Several patient series have shown benefits in
                  stimulating various brain areas, including the sensory
                  thalamus (ventral posterior lateral and medial), the
                  periaqueductal and periventricular grey, or, more recently,
                  the anterior cingulate cortex. However, this technique remains
                  “off label” in the USA as it does not have Federal Drug
                  Administration approval. Consequently, only a small number of
                  surgeons report DBS for pain using current technology and
                  techniques and few regions approve it. Randomised, blinded and
                  controlled clinical trials that may use novel trial
                  methodologies are desirable to evaluate the efficacy of DBS in
                  patients who are refractory to other therapies. New imaging
                  techniques, including tractography, may help optimise
                  electrode placement and clinical outcome.},
