                  -16.6 to -7.4]; P < .001). Psilocybin treatment was also
                  associated with significantly reduced Sheehan Disability Scale
                  scores compared with niacin (mean difference, -2.31 [95\% CI,
                  3.50-1.11]; P < .001) from baseline to day 43. More
                  participants receiving psilocybin had sustained response (but
                  not remission) than those receiving niacin. There were no
                  serious treatment-emergent AEs; however, psilocybin treatment
                  was associated with a higher rate of overall AEs and a higher
                  rate of severe AEs. CONCLUSIONS AND RELEVANCE: Psilocybin
                  treatment was associated with a clinically significant
                  sustained reduction in depressive symptoms and functional
                  disability, without serious adverse events. These findings add
                  to increasing evidence that psilocybin-when administered with
