  issue        = {12},
  pages        = {719--732},
  date         = {2015-12},
  abstract     = {Classically, the basal ganglia have been considered to have a
                  role in producing habitual and goal-directed behaviours. In
                  this article, we review recent evidence that expands this
                  role, indicating that the basal ganglia are also involved in
                  neural and behavioural inhibition in the motor and non-motor
                  domains. We then distinguish between goal-directed and
                  habitual (also known as automatic) inhibition mediated by
                  fronto-striato-subthalamic-pallido-thalamo-cortical networks.
