                  phenomena–seeing others act or believing that collective
                  awareness is rising (“constructive hope”). Hope for some,
                  however, stems from the belief that God or nature will solve
                  the problem without the need for human intervention (which we
                  call “false hope”). The most prevalent doubts are low
                  prioritization, greed, and intergroup conflict (i.e., the need
                  for cooperation at various scales to successfully address the
                  issue). We identified both “constructive” and “fatalistic”
                  doubts. Constructive doubts are concerns that humanity won't
                  address the problem effectively, while fatalistic doubts are
                  beliefs that we can't address the problem even if we wanted to
                  because it is in the hands of God or Mother Nature. In study
