                  and Courtine, Grégoire},
  journaltitle = {Nature},
  volume       = {563},
  issue        = {7729},
  pages        = {65--71},
  date         = {2018},
  abstract     = {Spinal cord injury leads to severe locomotor deficits or even
                  complete leg paralysis. Here we introduce targeted spinal cord
                  stimulation neurotechnologies that enabled voluntary control
                  of walking in individuals who had sustained a spinal cord
                  injury more than four years ago and presented with permanent
                  motor deficits or complete paralysis despite extensive
                  rehabilitation. Using an implanted pulse generator with
                  real-time triggering capabilities, we delivered trains of
                  spatially selective stimulation to the lumbosacral spinal cord
                  with timing that coincided with the intended movement. Within
                  one week, this spatiotemporal stimulation had re-established
