  abstract     = {Huntingtin (HTT) is now a famous protein because an abnormal
                  expansion of a glutamine stretch (polyQ) in its N-terminal
                  sequence leads to the devastating neurodegenerative disorder
                  Huntington's disease (HD). The gene encoding huntingtin, HTT,
                  and its dominantly inherited mutation were identified more
                  than 20 years ago. Subsequently, in the hope of finding a cure
                  for HD, there has been intense research aimed at understanding
                  the molecular mechanisms underlying the deleterious effects of
                  the presence of the abnormal polyQ expansion in HTT.
                  Notwithstanding with the value of this approach, evidence has
                  been emerging of a potential role of context and function of
                  the HTT protein in the specificity and severity of the
                  pathogenicity. HTT is ubiquitous both at the tissue and
