  issue        = {2},
  pages        = {83--90},
  date         = {2018},
  abstract     = {Background: Asleep deep brain stimulation (aDBS) implantation
                  replaces microelectrode recording for image-guided
                  implantation, shortening the operative time and reducing
                  cerebrospinal fluid egress. This may decrease pneumocephalus,
                  thus decreasing brain shift during implantation. Objective: To
                  compare the incidence and volume of pneumocephalus during
                  awake (wkDBS) and aDBS procedures. Methods: A retrospective
                  review of bilateral DBS cases performed at Oregon Health \&
                  Science University from 2009 to 2017 was undertaken.
                  Postimplantation imaging was reviewed to determine the
                  presence and volume of intracranial air and measure cortical
                  brain shift. Results: Among 371 patients, pneumocephalus was
                  noted in 66\% of wkDBS and 15.6\% of aDBS. The average volume
                  of air was significantly higher in wkDBS than aDBS (8.0 vs.
                  1.8 mL). Volumes of air greater than 7 mL, which have
                  previously been linked to brain shift, occurred significantly
                  more frequently in wkDBS than aDBS (34 vs 5.6\%). wkDBS
                  resulted in significantly larger cortical brain shifts (5.8
                  vs. 1.2 mm). Conclusions: We show that aDBS reduces the
                  incidence of intracranial air, larger air volumes, and
                  cortical brain shift. Large volumes of intracranial air have
                  been correlated to shifting of brain structures during DBS
                  procedures, a variable that could impact accuracy of electrode
                  placement.}
}

