  date         = {2016},
  abstract     = {Self-grooming is a complex innate behaviour with an
                  evolutionarily conserved sequencing pattern and is one of the
                  most frequently performed behavioural activities in rodents.
                  In this Review, we discuss the neurobiology of rodent
                  self-grooming, and we highlight studies of rodent models of
                  neuropsychiatric disorders — including models of autism
                  spectrum disorder and obsessive compulsive disorder — that
                  have assessed self-grooming phenotypes. We suggest that rodent
                  self-grooming may be a useful measure of repetitive behaviour
