  journaltitle = {EMBO Mol. Med.},
  pages        = {e9575},
  date         = {2019},
  abstract     = {Deep brain stimulation (DBS) has been successfully used to
                  treat movement disorders, such as Parkinson's disease, for
                  more than 25 years and heralded the advent of electrical
                  neuromodulation to treat diseases with dysregulated neuronal
                  circuits. DBS is now superseding ablative techniques, such as
                  stereotactic radiofrequency lesions. While serendipity has
                  played a role in developing DBS as a therapy, research during
                  the past two decades has shown that electrical neuromodulation
                  is far more than a functional lesion that can be switched on
                  and off. This understanding broadens the field to enable new
                  types of stimulation, clinical indications, and research. This
                  review highlights the complex effects of DBS from the single
                  cell to the neuronal network. Specifically, we examine the
