  journaltitle = {Neuroimage},
  volume       = {184},
  pages        = {293--316},
  date         = {2018},
  abstract     = {Deep brain stimulation (DBS) is a highly efficacious treatment
                  option for movement disorders and a growing number of other
                  indications are investigated in clinical trials. To ensure
                  optimal treatment outcome, exact electrode placement is
                  required. Moreover, to analyze the relationship between
                  electrode location and clinical results, a precise
                  reconstruction of electrode placement is required, posing
                  specific challenges to the field of neuroimaging. Since 2014
                  the open source toolbox Lead-DBS is available, which aims at
                  facilitating this process. The tool has since become a popular
                  platform for DBS imaging. With support of a broad community of
                  researchers worldwide, methods have been continuously updated
                  and complemented by new tools for tasks such as multispectral
                  nonlinear registration, structural/functional connectivity
                  analyses, brain shift correction, reconstruction of
                  microelectrode recordings and orientation detection of
