                  maintain a steady gait, with low stride-to-stride variability
                  of gait cycle timing and its subphases, would be diminished
                  with both Parkinson's disease (PD) and Huntington's disease
                  (HD). To test this hypothesis, we obtained quantitative
                  measures of stride-to-stride variability of gait cycle timing
                  in subjects with PD (n = 15), HD (n = 20), and disease-free
                  controls (n = 16). All measures of gait variability were
                  significantly increased in PD and HD. In subjects with PD and
                  HD, gait variability measures were two and three times that
