  journaltitle = {Nature},
  pages        = {1--9},
  date         = {2021-06-30},
  abstract     = {The ability to ‘sense’ the social environment and thereby to
                  understand the thoughts and actions of others allows humans to
                  fit into their social worlds, communicate and cooperate, and
                  learn from others’ experiences. Here we argue that, through
                  the lens of computational social science, this ability can be
                  used to advance research into human sociality. When
                  strategically selected to represent a specific population of
                  interest, human social sensors can help to describe and
                  predict societal trends. In addition, their reports of how
                  they experience their social worlds can help to build models
                  of social dynamics that are constrained by the empirical
                  reality of human social systems. The ability of people to
