  volume       = {10},
  pages        = {431},
  date         = {2016},
  abstract     = {This study describes a cost-effective screening protocol for
                  parkinsonism based on combined objective and subjective
                  monitoring of balance function. Objective evaluation of
                  balance function was performed using a game industry balance
                  board and an automated analyses of the dynamic of the center
                  of pressure in time, frequency, and non-linear domains
                  collected during short series of stand up tests with different
                  modalities and severity of sensorial deprivation. The
                  subjective measurement of balance function was performed using
                  the Dizziness Handicap Inventory questionnaire. Principal
                  component analyses on both objective and subjective
                  measurements of balance function allowed to obtained a
                  specificity and selectivity for parkinsonian patients (vs.
