                  has shown to be effective in improving most parkinsonian
                  signs, particularly the levodopa-responsive ones. However, its
                  effect on axial signs (such as balance, gait, speech, or
                  swallowing) may not be sustained, minimal, or even
                  detrimental. For these reasons, several studies have explored
                  the effectiveness of low-frequency stimulation (generally 60
                  or 80 Hz). Methods, results, and especially interpretations of
                  these studies are quite variable. Although the use of
                  low-frequency stimulation certainly opens new avenues in the
                  field of deep brain stimulation, after having gathered all the
                  available evidence in patients with subthalamic implants, our
