  date         = {2018},
  abstract     = {The ventral intermediate nucleus (VIM) of the thalamus is an
                  established surgical target for stereotactic ablation and deep
                  brain stimulation (DBS) in the treatment of tremor in
                  Parkinson's disease (PD) and essential tremor (ET). It is
                  centrally placed on a cerebello-thalamo-cortical network
                  connecting the primary motor cortex, to the dentate nucleus of
                  the contralateral cerebellum through the
                  dentato-rubro-thalamic tract (DRT). The VIM is not readily
                  visible on conventional MR imaging, so identifying the
