                  particular, MEG may offer additional insight not possible by
                  MRI when used to study complex eloquent function, where the
                  precise timing and coordination of brain areas is critical.
                  This article will review the traditional use of MEG for
                  functional neurosurgery, describe recent advances in MEG
                  connectivity analyses, and consider the additional benefits
                  that could be gained with the inclusion of MEG connectivity
                  studies. Since MEG has been most widely applied to the study
                  of epilepsy, we will frame this article within the context of
