  issue        = {2},
  pages        = {113--125},
  date         = {2020-03},
  abstract     = {Introduction: It has been proposed that hallucinations occur
                  because of problems with reality discrimination (when
                  internal, self-generated cognitions are misattributed to an
                  external, non-self source) and because of elevated levels of
                  top-down processing. In this study, we examined whether visual
                  reality discrimination abilities and elevated top-down
                  processing (assessed via face pareidolia-proneness) were
