                  = 82, mean age = 23.12 years) completed a visual reality
                  discrimination task and a face pareidolia task, as well as
                  self-report measures of schizotypy and of the frequency of
                  visual hallucination-like experiences.Results: Regression
                  analysis demonstrated that the number of false alarms made on
                  the visual reality discrimination task and the number of hits
                  made on the face pareidolia task were independent predictors
                  of the frequency of visual hallucination-like experiences.
                  Correlations between performance on the tasks and levels of
                  schizotypy were not statistically significant.Conclusions:
                  These findings suggest that weaker visual reality
                  discrimination abilities and elevated levels of top-down
                  processing are associated with visual hallucination-proneness
                  and are discussed in terms of the idea that clinical visual
