                  entrepreneurial research. However, this is the first
                  comprehensive review of the studies published in this field
                  and therefore describes the current state of the art of
                  cognitive bias research in entrepreneurship. This paper
                  identified a total of 54 relevant articles and structures them
                  by a typology of bias. It shows that a considerable number of
                  articles cover overoptimism and overconfidence, while limited
                  attention has been given to other biases such as illusion of
                  control, the belief in the law of small numbers, escalation of
                  commitment, planning fallacy, status quo bias, self-serving
                  bias, and hindsight bias. Results of this review show that
                  studies examining overoptimism and overconfidence have
                  delivered most consistent results. The focus of the review
                  lies on logically structuring the body of research by
                  assessing the key issues in the determinants and in the
