  date         = {2018},
  abstract     = {Usually, we do not question that we possess a body and act
                  upon the world. This pre-reflective awareness of being a
                  bodily and agentive self can, however, be disrupted by
                  different clinical conditions. Whereas sense of ownership
                  (SoO) describes the feeling of mineness toward one’s own body
                  parts, feelings or thoughts, sense of agency (SoA) refers to
                  the experience of initiating and controlling an action.
                  Although SoA and SoO naturally coincide, both experiences can
                  also be made in isolation. By using many different
                  experimental paradigms, both experiences have been extensively
                  studied over the last years. This review introduces both
                  concepts, with a special focus also onto their interplay.
