                  function. The local microcircuit amplifies long-distance
                  inter-areal input, which show distance-dependent changes in
                  their laminar profiles. Statistical modeling of these changes
                  in laminar profiles demonstrates that inputs from multiple
                  hierarchical levels to their target areas show remarkable
                  consistency, allowing the construction of a cortical hierarchy
                  based on a principle of hierarchical distance. The statistical
                  modeling that is applied to structure can also be applied to
                  laminar differences in the oscillatory coherence between areas
                  thereby determining a functional hierarchy of the cortex.
                  Close examination of the anatomy of inter-areal connectivity
                  reveals a dual counterstream architecture with well-defined
                  distance-dependent feedback and feedforward pathways in both
                  the supra- and infragranular layers, suggesting a multiplicity
                  of feedback pathways with well-defined functional properties.
