  pages        = {1748--1751},
  date         = {2016-11},
  abstract     = {OBJECTIVE: Beta band oscillations in the subthalamic nucleus
                  (STN) have been proposed as a pathophysiological signature in
                  patients with Parkinson's disease (PD). The aim of this study
                  was to investigate the potential association between
                  oscillatory activity in the STN and symptom severity in PD.
                  METHODS: Subthalamic local field potentials were recorded from
                  63 PD patients in a dopaminergic OFF state. Power-spectra were
                  analyzed for the frequency range from 5 to 95 Hz and
                  correlated with individual UPDRS-III motor scores in the OFF
                  state. RESULTS: A correlation between total UPDRS-III scores
                  and 8 to 35 Hz activity was revealed across all patients (ρ =
                  0.44, P < .0001). When correlating each frequency bin, a
                  narrow range from 10 to 15 Hz remained significant for the
                  correlation (false discovery rate corrected P < .05).
