  issue        = {8},
  pages        = {3241--3252},
  date         = {2018-08},
  abstract     = {Finding creative solutions to difficult problems is a
                  fundamental aspect of human culture and a skill highly needed.
                  However, the exact neural processes underlying creative
                  problem solving remain unclear. Insightful problem solving
                  tasks were shown to be a valid method for investigating one
                  subcomponent of creativity: the Aha!-moment. Finding
                  insightful solutions during a remote associates task (RAT) was
                  found to elicit specific cortical activity changes.
                  Considering the strong affective components of Aha!-moments,
                  as manifested in the subjectively experienced feeling of
                  relief following the sudden emergence of the solution of the
                  problem without any conscious forewarning, we hypothesized the
                  subcortical dopaminergic reward network to be critically
                  engaged during Aha. To investigate those subcortical
                  contributions to insight, we employed ultra-high-field 7 T
