  volume       = {18},
  issue        = {4},
  date         = {2021-08-31},
  abstract     = {Objective. Technical advances in deep brain stimulation (DBS)
                  are crucial to improve therapeutic efficacy and battery life.
                  We report the potentialities and pitfalls of one of the first
                  commercially available devices capable of recording brain
                  local field potentials (LFPs) from the implanted DBS leads,
                  chronically and during stimulation. The aim was to provide
                  clinicians with well-grounded tips on how to maximize the
                  capabilities of this novel device, both in everyday practice
                  and for research purposes.Approach. We collected clinical and
                  neurophysiological data of the first 20 patients (14 with
                  Parkinson's disease (PD), five with dystonia, one with chronic
                  pain) that received the Percept™ PC in our centres. We also
                  performed tests in a saline bath to validate the recordings
                  quality.Main results. The Percept PC reliably recorded the LFP
                  of the implanted site, wirelessly and in real time. We
