                  illuminates several issues in cognitive and clinical
                  neuroscience with implications for experiences of selfhood and
                  emotion. We first contextualize interoception in terms of
                  active (Bayesian) inference in the brain, highlighting its
                  enactivist (embodied) aspects. We then consider the key role
                  of uncertainty or precision and how this might translate into
                  neuromodulation. We next examine the implications for
                  understanding the functional anatomy of the emotional brain,
                  surveying recent observations on agranular cortex. Finally, we
                  turn to theoretical issues, namely, the role of interoception
                  in shaping a sense of embodied self and feelings. We will draw
