  date         = {2008-06-01},
  abstract     = {Abstract Why have humans, throughout history and across
                  cultures, shown a strong tendency to believe in the existence
                  of superhuman intentional agents and attached this belief to
                  notions of morality, misfortune, and the creation of the
                  world? The answer emerging from the cognitive science of
                  religion appears to be that explicit beliefs are informed and
                  constrained by the natural and cross-culturally recurrent
                  operation of implicit cognitive systems. Successful god
                  concepts resonate with the expectations of these implicit
                  systems but also have attention-demanding and
                  inferentially-rich properties that allow their integration
                  into various areas of human concern. Theological concepts may
                  deviate from these natural cognitive moorings but require
                  special cultural scaffolding, such as Whitehouse's two Modes
