  date         = {2022-05-06},
  abstract     = {Background Pathologically increased beta power has been
                  described as a biomarker for Parkinson’s disease (PD) and
                  related to prolonged bursts of subthalamic beta
                  synchronization. Methods Here, we investigate the association
                  between subthalamic beta dynamics and motor impairment in a
                  cohort of 106 Parkinson’s patients in the ON- and
                  OFF-medication state, suing two different methods of beta
                  burst determination. Results We report a frequency-specific
                  correlation of low beta power and burst duration with motor
                  impairment OFF dopaminergic medication. Furthermore, reduction
                  of power and burst duration correlated significantly with
