                  the experience of episodic memory corresponds to a "jump back
                  in time" in which a previous state of temporal context is
                  recovered. We suggest that these 2 sets of findings could be
                  different facets of a representation of temporal history that
                  maintains a record at the last few thousand seconds of
                  experience. The ability to represent long time scales comes at
                  the cost of discarding precise information about when a
                  stimulus was experienced--this uncertainty becomes greater for
                  events further in the past. We review recent computational
                  work that describes a mechanism that could construct such a
                  scale-invariant representation. Taken as a whole, this
                  suggests the hippocampus plays its role in multiple aspects of
