  date         = {1987},
  abstract     = {Stereotactic thalamotomy of the thalamic nucleus ventralis
                  intermedius (VIM) is routinely used for movement disorders.
                  During this procedure, it has been observed that
                  high-frequency (100 Hz) stimulation of VIM was able to stop
                  the extrapyramidal tremor. In patients with bilateral tremor
                  of extrapyramidal origin, who were resistant to drug therapy,
                  the therapeutic protocol associated (1) a radiofrequency VIM
                  thalamotomy for the most disabled side, and (2) a continuous
                  VIM stimulation for the other side using stereotactically
                  implanted electrodes, connected to subcutaneous stimulators.
                  VIM thalamotomy relieved the tremor in all operated cases.
