  journaltitle = {Front. Neurol.},
  volume       = {12},
  pages        = {765203},
  date         = {2021-10-28},
  abstract     = {Sleep disturbances, specifically decreases in total sleep time
                  and sleep efficiency as well as increased sleep onset latency
                  and wakefulness after sleep onset, are highly prevalent in
                  patients with Parkinson's disease (PD). Impairment of sleep
                  significantly and adversely impacts several comorbidities in
                  this patient population, including cognition, mood, and
                  quality of life. Sleep disturbances and other non-motor
                  symptoms of PD have come to the fore as the effectiveness of
                  advanced therapies such as deep brain stimulation (DBS)
                  optimally manage the motor symptoms. Although some studies
                  have suggested that DBS provides benefit for sleep
                  disturbances in PD, the mechanisms by which this might occur,
                  as well as the optimal stimulation parameters for treating
                  sleep dysfunction, remain unknown. In patients treated with
                  DBS, electrophysiologic recording from the stimulating
                  electrode, in the form of local field potentials (LFPs), has
                  led to the identification of several findings associated with
                  both motor and non-motor symptoms including sleep. For
                  example, beta frequency (13-30 Hz) oscillations are associated
                  with worsened bradykinesia while awake and decrease during
                  non-rapid eye movement sleep. LFP investigation of sleep has
                  largely focused on the subthalamic nucleus (STN), though
                  corresponding oscillatory activity has been found in the
