  issue        = {1431},
  pages        = {517--528},
  date         = {2003-03-29},
  abstract     = {It has been proposed that the capacity to code the 'like me'
                  analogy between self and others constitutes a basic
                  prerequisite and a starting point for social cognition. It is
                  by means of this self/other equivalence that meaningful social
                  bonds can be established, that we can recognize others as
                  similar to us, and that imitation can take place. In this
                  article I discuss recent neurophysiological and brain imaging
                  data on monkeys and humans, showing that the 'like me' analogy
                  may rest upon a series of 'mirror-matching' mechanisms. A new
                  conceptual tool able to capture the richness of the
                  experiences we share with others is introduced: the shared
