  volume       = {20},
  issue        = {1},
  pages        = {115--131},
  date         = {2007-02-01},
  abstract     = {The 19th-century story of Phineas Gage is much quoted in
                  neuroscientific literature as the first recorded case in which
                  personality change (from polite and sociable to psychopathic)
                  occurred after damage to the brain. In this article I contest
                  this interpretation. From a close examination of the story of
                  Gage I have come to conclude that first of all there was
                  nothing psychopathic in Gage?s behavior and that changes in
                  his life are more coherently explained by seeing them as his
                  way of dealing with disfigurement that he suffered after the
                  accident. This is not just a matter of reinterpreting a case.
                  The way Gage has been presented and discussed in
                  neuroscientific literature suggests that the new paradigm of
                  neuroscientifically oriented psychiatry may lead to an erosion
                  of clinical knowledge.}
}

