  pages        = {487--494},
  date         = {1979-12},
  abstract     = {Theories of spatial cognition are derived from many sources.
                  Psychologists are concerned with determining the features of
                  the mind which, in combination with external inputs, produce
                  our spatialized experience. A review of philosophical and
                  other approaches has convinced us that the brain must come
                  equipped to impose a three-dimensional Euclidean framework on
                  experience – our analysis suggests that object
                  re-identification may require such a framework. We identify
                  this absolute, nonegocentric, spatial framework with a
                  specific neural system centered in the hippocampus.A
                  consideration of the kinds of behaviours in which such a
                  spatial mapping system would be important is followed by an
                  analysis of the anatomy and physiology of this system, with
                  special emphasis on the place-coded neurons recorded in the
                  hippocampus of freely moving rats. A tentative physiological
                  model for the hippocampal cognitive map is proposed. A review
                  of lesion studies, in tasks as diverse as discrimination
                  learning, avoidance, and extinction, shows that the cognitive
                  map notion can adequately explain much of the data.The model
                  is extended to humans by the assumption that spatial maps are
                  built in one hemisphere, semantic maps in the other. The
                  latter provide a semantic deep structure within which
                  discourse comprehension and production can be achieved.
