  abstract  = {The thalamus is a small, bilateral structure in the diencephalon
               that integrates signals from many areas of the CNS. This critical
               anatomical position allows the thalamus to influence whole-brain
               activity and adaptive behaviour. However, traditional research
               paradigms have struggled to attribute specific functions to the
               thalamus, and it has remained understudied in the human
               neuroimaging literature. Recent advances in analytical techniques
               and increased accessibility to large, high-quality data sets have
               brought forth a series of studies and findings that
               (re-)establish the thalamus as a core region of interest in human
               cognitive neuroscience, a field that otherwise remains
               cortico-centric. In this Perspective, we argue that using
               whole-brain neuroimaging approaches to investigate the thalamus
               and its interaction with the rest of the brain is key for
               understanding systems-level control of information processing. To
               this …}
